  "text": "Question ten. When would you use a Poisson instead of a binomial? A binomial needs a fixed number of trials, each with the same chance: a thousand impressions, each clicked or not. A Poisson counts events with no natural ceiling, like arrivals per minute. And the two meet. A thousand trials at point three percent is almost exactly a Poisson with a rate of three. Then two puzzles, and the room from the start.\n\nQuestion eleven. Explain the Monty Hall problem. Three doors, and a car behind one. You pick a door. The host, who knows where the car is, opens another one onto a goat, and offers you the switch. Take it. Your first pick was right one time in three. The host never opens the car, so the other two thirds all land on the one door he left shut. A hundred thousand games: staying wins thirty-three percent, switching sixty-seven.\n\nQuestion twelve, the room from the start. Twenty-three people: is a shared birthday more likely than not? Yes, just. People don't collide, pairs do, and twenty-three people make two hundred and fifty-three pairs. The exact chance is fifty point seven percent, and fifty thousand simulated rooms say the same. By fifty-seven people, it's over ninety-nine.",
